issue: If `img.complete` is true but `img.naturalWidth === 0` (e.g., a broken image or empty `src`), this branch is taken and listeners are attached, but neither `load` nor `error` will ever fire on an already-`complete` image. The promise will hang and only resolve via the outer 5s timeout (this issue also existed in the previous code, but it is now slightly easier to hit because the awaited `decode()` adds another step that can stall on broken images).

Consider gating only the load wait on `!img.complete`, then conditionally decoding only on success:

```javascript
if (!img.complete) {
    await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
        img.addEventListener('load', resolve, { once: true });
        img.addEventListener('error', reject, { once: true });
    });
}
if (img.naturalWidth > 0) {
    await img.decode();
}
```

