review_comment:
@skshetry Yeah, but this is just cache and we can't encode everything into the token (e.g. root_dir), so we go the reverse route by just using a token here, but `dvc doctor` will tell you precisely the location of your cache.

I'm open to suggestions actually. Putting just salt seems odd, because it is just salt and we have more useful components like user/root/major version.

I'm happy to change it now or in the future.

@pmrowla The reason for that was that the `describe` utility types scm as `"Git"` but it receives an object typed `"Git | NoSCM"` from the way [scm is defined in the Repo class ](https://github.com/tibor-mach/dvc/blob/9eb41264fec9d3e4fc50f55f4345e23048c0a2be/dvc/repo/__init__.py#L147-L148).

So mypy doesn't like that when I run a pre-commit check. I haven't really used typing much before so I am not sure what the best practice is here. I added the check mostly to satisfy mypy :-)

How should I address the mypy error then? It didn't seem correct to me to use `Optional[Union["Git", "NoSCM"]]` in the definition of `describe` because you definitely need an SCM to make it run properly.

So to set a completely new remote, you would need something like:

```python
with dvc.api.open(
    "data",
    remote="newremote",
    config={
        "remote": {
            "newremote": {
                "url": ...,
                ...
            }
        }
    }
) as f:
```

Can we add `remote_config` so it works like this?

```python
with dvc.api.open(
    "data",
    remote="newremote",
    remote_config={
        "url": ...,
        ...
    }
) as f:
```

`remote` sets the default remote, and `remote_config` merges with the config for the default remote. If you need control of additional remotes, you can use `config`.

@dberenbaum I would rather just:

```python
with dvc.api.open(
    "data",
    config={
        "core": {"remote": "newremote"},
        "remote": {
            "newremote": {
                "url": ...,
                ...
            }
        }
    }
) as f:
```

because that's how you would do it in your .dvc/config[.local]

@dberenbaum Yeah, I totally get the `remote_config` thing and I did consider it, but opted for a quicker `config` solution for now, since we have everything in place to use it. I guess dvcfs could stay the same, but api would definitely benefit from `remote_config`.

Would you rather not add `config` at all to the api methods for now? (still not too late to remove, it wasn't released yet)

My logic with `remote` is not that I'm familair with remote structure but rather that users are familiar with dvc config, since that's the way they would be changing stuff manually. E.g.

```python
config = {
        "core": {"remote": "newremote"},
        "remote": {
            "newremote": {
                "url": ...,
                ...
            }
        }
 }
```

is the same as

```ini
[core]
    remote = newremote
    
[remote "newremote"]
    url = ...
```

> config is the most powerful mechanism that can allow one to get out of very sticky situations

@efiop, I think you are looking at it from a maintainer's point of view rather than the user. We can provide powerful mechanism in `DvcFileSystem`, `read/open` should be simpler. `remote_config=` solves most of the problem imo.

Creating a new remote, and/or providing a way to configure `dvc` repo is more of a niche usecase. Also, I don't feel comfortable exposing the whole config schema to the users. It has a large surface area, and can have unintended effect when not use correctly.

