In the pure-Python implementation, `Segment.start`/`Segment.end` are writable attributes, but the C `unicodedata.Segment` exposes them as read-only members. If this module is meant to be a behavioral match for `unicodedata.iter_graphemes()`, consider making `start`/`end` read-only (e.g., store `_start`/`_end` and expose `@property` accessors without setters) to avoid surprising mutation of segment boundaries affecting `str(segment)`/`repr(segment)`.
```suggestion
    __slots__ = ('_string', '_start', '_end')

    def __init__(self, string, start, end):
        self._string = string
        self._start = start
        self._end = end

    @property
    def start(self):
        return self._start

    @property
    def end(self):
        return self._end
```

